  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nA complex, amplified upper air pattern will dictate the extended \nforecast. Rain chances will loom for much of the period, including \nthrough the weekend to start. Timing differences still remain for \nSaturday on when precipitation will pause, but for now, it appears a \nwarm front will lift northward towards Minnesota through the day, \nbut there is enough uncertainty in remaining moisture to warrant \nkeep a ~30% chance of rain in the area for Halloween night. Lee \ncyclogenesis will temporarily pull away moisture from the area \nSunday, but it will return with a large closed system to start next \nweek. Location of the warm sector is still uncertain as the \nformation of the closed low involves the combination of a couple \ncomponent waves merging together. What can be said is the resultant \nfeature will become wound up in the central US and will result in an \nextended period of rain chances, cloud cover, and cooler \ntemperatures for the first week of November. It's possible that \nhighs next week don't reach 70 degrees for the area.",
